{
  "dom_id": "step-q44781",
  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "Represent the solution of the inequality $-2x+3\\le 7$ on a number line.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we apply the addition principle, subtracting $3$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+3 & \\le 7 \\\\ -2x+3-3 & \\le 7-3 \\\\ -2x+0 & \\le 4 \\\\ -2x & \\le 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing by $-2.$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,4 \\\\ \\frac{-2x}{-2}\\, & \\ge \\,\\,\\frac{4}{-2} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{-2}x}{\\cancel{-2}}\\, & \\ge \\,\\,-2 \\\\ x\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\ge -2.$ The solution can be expressed on a number line, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14192536/assets/b92ce4122ef412dd99aedba0bd4d01449dc1bf4ad2a9e40bb8894ebdd8fa3593.png)",
